For the fixed power $p$ and index $k$ in Theorem <a href="#thm:blowup" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:blowup">1</a>, and for every $\alpha>k+6$, the initial datum $$\label{eq:gaussian}
 u_0^\omega(x)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z^{12}}
       g_n(\omega)\langle n\rangle^{-\alpha}e^{in\cdot x}$$ belongs to $H^k$ almost surely and has positive probability of finite-time blowup. In particular, the following assertion is false: for every energy-supercritical pair $(d,p)$ there is a finite $\alpha_0(d,p)$ such that, for every $\alpha>\alpha_0(d,p)$ and every $$d/2+2<m<\alpha-d/2,$$ the local $H^m$ solution with the Gaussian datum extends almost surely to $C(\mathbb R;H^m(\mathbb T^d))$.

For $\operatorname{Re}q>-1$, $\operatorname{Re}x\ge0$, $x\ne0$, define, with principal powers, $$\label{free:H-definition}
 H(q,m,x)=x^{-q}\left\{1+\frac1{\Gamma(q)}
 \int_0^\infty e^{-u}u^{q-1}
 \left[(1+u/x)^{m-1-q}-1\right]\,du\right\}.$$ Here $m$ may in particular be any positive integer used below. This definition is holomorphic in $q$, including $q=0$, and in $x$ on the interior of the indicated region, with holomorphic continuation across each nonzero point of its imaginary boundary. Primes on $H$ denote derivatives in its last argument. It satisfies $$\label{free:H-equation}
 xH''+(m-x)H'-qH=0,\qquad
 H'(q,m,x)=-qH(q+1,m+1,x),\qquad H(0,m,x)=1.$$ For every $N\ge1$ it has the expansion $$\label{free:H-expansion}
 H(q,m,x)=x^{-q}\left\{
 \sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\frac{(-1)^j(q)_j(1+q-m)_j}{j!x^j}
 +O(|x|^{-N})\right\}.$$ Here $(q)_0=1$ and $(q)_j=\prod_{n=0}^{j-1}(q+n)$ for $j\ge1$. The expansion is uniform for $\operatorname{Re}x\ge0$, $|x|\ge1$, and compact parameter sets. The same remainder estimate holds after any fixed number of $x\partial_x$ derivatives of the bracket. The symbol expansion specifies the slow solution, without an oscillatory component on the imaginary rays.

Let $L_n(t)=e^t\partial_t^n(e^{-t}t^n)/n!$ be the Laguerre polynomials, orthonormal for $e^{-t}\,dt$ on $[0,\infty)$. Write $$G=\sum_{n\ge0}g_nL_n,\qquad
 B_n=\sum_{k\ge n}g_k,\qquad C_n=\sum_{k\ge n}(k-n)g_k.$$ These series and their parameter derivatives converge rapidly. With $t_n=q+n$ and $d_n=t_n-M$, one has $$\begin{aligned}
 &(B_0,C_0)=(G(0),-G'(0)),\label{free:initial-tail}\\
 &t_ng_n=-MB_{n+1}-sC_n,\qquad
 d_nB_{n+1}=t_nB_n+sC_n,\qquad
 C_{n+1}=C_n-B_{n+1}.\label{free:recurrence}
\end{aligned}$$ The real quadratic form $$\label{free:cone-form}
 p_n=\frac M2|B_n|^2+\operatorname{Re}(s\overline{B_n}C_n)$$ satisfies the exact identity $$\label{free:cone-increment}
 p_{n+1}-p_n=
 (\operatorname{Re}\lambda+n-5/2)|g_n|^2.$$

Define $$\label{free:backward}
 F_n=\begin{pmatrix}d_n-s&-s\\t_n&t_n\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 P_K=F_0F_1\cdots F_{K-1}.$$ Then, including parameters where an individual $t_n$ or $d_n$ vanishes, $$\label{free:column-identity}
 P_K\begin{pmatrix}R_h\\1\end{pmatrix}
 =\frac1{\widehat C_K}
 \begin{pmatrix}H(q,M+1,-s)\\-H'(q,M+1,-s)\end{pmatrix}.$$ The scalar on the right is holomorphic and nonzero. The column is nonzero.

The first conclusion of Proposition <a href="#free:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="free:main">3</a> holds. More precisely there is an invertible real-linear map $\mathcal L:\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb C$ with negative determinant and minimum stretch greater than $.048$ such that $$\label{free:profile-linearization}
 |j(b,Z)-\mathcal L(b-b_*,Z-Z_*)|<2.5\cdot10^{-11}
 \qquad((b,Z)\in\mathcal D).$$ In particular $|j|>4.55\cdot10^{-10}$ on $\partial\mathcal D$.

There is $a_0>0$ such that, for every $0<a<a_0$, there are $(b_a,Z_a)\in\mathcal D$ and a smooth nonvanishing radial stationary solution $Q_a=A_ae^{i\phi_a}$ of <a href="#profile:similarity" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="profile:similarity">[profile:similarity]</a>, normalized by $Q_a(0)>0$. They have the following properties.*


1.  *The identities <a href="#profile:amplitude" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="profile:amplitude">[profile:amplitude]</a>–<a href="#profile:divergence" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="profile:divergence">[profile:divergence]</a> hold, $0<P_a\le1$, and there are constants $c,C>0$, independent of small $a$, such that $$\label{profile:coercive-bounds}
     C^{-1}\langle r\rangle^{-4a}\le\mu_a(r)
           \le C\langle r\rangle^{-4a},\qquad
     \nabla w_a\ge c I.$$*


2.  *If $a_n\downarrow0$ and $(b_{a_n},Z_{a_n})\to(b,Z)$, then $j(b,Z)=0$, where $j$ is the free matching function of Proposition <a href="#free:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="free:main">3</a>. With $R=2\sqrt Z$, the profiles converge in $C^1$ on every compact ball to $$\label{profile:limit}
     Q_0(r)=
     \begin{cases}
     1,&0\le r\le R,\\[2pt]
     \displaystyle\frac{H(-ib,6,-ir^2/4)}{H(-ib,6,-iZ)},&r\ge R.
     \end{cases}$$ The exterior amplitude is strictly decreasing and has a positive limit at infinity. The convergence is $C^\infty$ on compact subsets of $\{r>R\}$. On every fixed ball, $$\label{profile:pressure-limit}
     P_{a_n}\ \stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup}\ b\mathbf1_{\{r<R\}}
           \quad\hbox{in }L^\infty,$$ and $\mu_{a_n},w_{a_n}$ converge in $C^1$. In particular $P_{a_n}/a_n\to0$ locally uniformly on $\{r>R\}$.*





3.  *On the fixed exterior $r\ge r_b$, $$\label{profile:tail}
     Q_a(r)=r^{\nu_a}f_a(r),\qquad \nu_a=-2a+2ib_a,
     \qquad 0<\kappa\le|f_a(r)|\le\rho<1,$$ with $\kappa,\rho$ independent of $a$. There are coefficients $m_{a,j}$, with $|m_{a,0}|\ge\kappa$, such that for every pair of fixed nonnegative integers $N,J$, $$\label{profile:symbol-expansion}
     \left|D^j\left(f_a(r)-\sum_{q=0}^{N}m_{a,q}r^{-2q}\right)\right|
           \le C_{N,J}r^{-2N-2},\qquad 0\le j\le J,\quad r\ge r_b.$$ The constants are uniform as $a\downarrow0$. In particular, $$\label{profile:exterior-pressure}
     |D^j(P_a/a)|\le\eta_j(a)r^{-2},\qquad
     |D^je^{2i\phi_a}|\le C_j,\qquad \eta_j(a)\longrightarrow0$$ for each fixed $j$. On the inner ball, $$\label{profile:pressure-transport}
     W_a(P_a/a)\le Ca(P_a/a),\qquad r\le r_b,$$ whereas $|W_a(P_a/a)|\le\eta(a)r^{-2}$ for $r\ge r_b$, with $\eta(a)\to0$. Consequently the positive part of $W_a(P_a/a)$ is bounded uniformly on the whole space.*



4.  *For each fixed $a$, every Cartesian derivative obeys $$\label{profile:fixed-power-symbols}
     |\partial^\beta Q_a(y)|\le C_{a,\beta}\langle y\rangle^{-2a-|\beta|},
     \qquad
     \left|\partial^\beta\big((P_a/a)e^{2i\phi_a}\big)\right|
           +|\partial^\beta(P_a/a)|
           \le C_{a,\beta}\langle y\rangle^{-2-|\beta|}.$$ The time-symmetry combination has the improved exterior order $$\label{profile:time-symbol}
     \left|D^j\left((a-ib_a+D/2)Q_a\right)\right|
           \le C_j r^{-2a-2},\qquad r\ge r_b.$$

For all sufficiently small $a>0$ and all $(b,Z)\in\mathcal D$, the system <a href="#profile:amplitude" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="profile:amplitude">[profile:amplitude]</a> has a positive radial solution on $[0,r_b]$ with $A'(0)=0$ and $A(r_b)=h_b$. Within the bounds $$\label{profile:inner-bounds}
 h_b\le A\le .5^a,$$ it is unique. It depends continuously on $(b,Z)$, and obeys $$\label{profile:bulk-barrier}
 A(r)\ge .2^a-.02(r-r_0)_+^2.$$ The amplitudes are bounded uniformly in $C^{1,1}[0,r_b]$.

Fix $\ell$ and a convergent profile subsequence. Let $R=2\sqrt Z$ be its limiting core radius, and fix a sufficiently large outer radius $B_2>r_b$. Let $E$ be the Hilbert space of $H^1$ pairs in this harmonic on the ball $r<B_2$, and let $$\mathscr Z=L^2(r<B_2;\mathbb C^2)\oplus\mathbb C^2,\qquad
 j_E X=(X,X(B_2)).$$ The map $j_E:E\to\mathscr Z$ is compact. Define $T_a:E'\to E$ by the coercive form $$\label{spec:base-form}
 \mathfrak b_a(X,\Theta)=
 \int\left(\nabla\Theta^*\nabla X+\Theta^*X
                         +(P/a)\overline{\Theta_f}f\right)\mu.$$ The dual $E'$ consists of conjugate-linear functionals on test vectors. Let $$E_0=\{(f,g)\in E:f=0\text{ a.e. on }r<R\},$$ and let $T_0:E'\to E_0$ be the inverse of the limiting form, with no pressure term and tests restricted to $E_0$. Then $$\label{spec:inverse-norm}
 \|j_ET_a-j_ET_0\|_{E'\to\mathscr Z}\longrightarrow0.$$

Near each bounded candidate $\lambda$, choose $B_2$ sufficiently large. The regular outgoing mode equation is equivalent to $$\label{spec:compact-equation}
 (I-\mathcal C_a(\lambda))z=0\quad\hbox{on }\mathscr Z,\qquad
 \mathcal C_a(\lambda)=j_ET_aK_a(\lambda).$$ These are compact holomorphic families, and $\mathcal C_a\to\mathcal C_0$ locally uniformly in operator norm.

The limiting kernel in <a href="#spec:compact-equation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="spec:compact-equation">[spec:compact-equation]</a> consists of free slow exterior modes satisfying $$\label{spec:matching}
 f(R)=0,\qquad g(R+)=g(R-),\qquad g'(R+)=g'(R-),\qquad
 g(r)=c r^\ell\quad(r<R).$$ It is nonzero exactly when the two-column determinant of Proposition <a href="#free:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="free:main">3</a> vanishes.

The embeddings $Y\hookrightarrow C_0(\mathbb R^{12})$ and $Y_L\hookrightarrow L^\infty(\mathbb T_L^{12})$ are bounded, uniformly for $L\ge1$. Both spaces are algebras, with a uniform algebra constant for $Y_L$. For a ball or annulus of radius $R\ge1$, contained in one torus cell when appropriate and with $R\le C_0L$, one has $$\label{lin:local-derivatives}
 \|\nabla^jv\|_{L^2(\text{ball or annulus})}
 \le C R^{\max(s_0-j,0)}\|v\|,
 \qquad 0\le j\le k.$$ Here and below an undecorated norm denotes the applicable $Y$ or $Y_L$ norm, and constants may depend on the fixed geometric constant $C_0$.*


*If $F$ satisfies $|\nabla^jF|\le C_j\langle y\rangle^{-\beta-j}$, then a smooth dyadic piece supported where $|y|\asymp R$ satisfies $$\label{lin:symbol-norm}
 \|\psi_RF\|\le C\big(R^{6-\beta-s_0}+R^{6-\beta-k}\big),$$ also on the tori for $R\le C_0L$. In particular $Q\in Y$, and for every fixed $\chi\in C_c^\infty(B_1)$ the cutoff profiles $\chi(y/L)Q(y)$ have uniformly bounded $Y_L$ norms.

For every $\varepsilon>0$ there are $R_\varepsilon,C_\varepsilon<\infty$ such that $$\label{lin:compact-bound}
 \|\mathcal V_*v\|_{s_0,*}
 +\|\nabla^k(\mathcal V_*v)-\mathcal V_*\nabla^kv\|_2
 \le\varepsilon\|v\|+C_\varepsilon\|v\|_{L^2(B_{R_\varepsilon})}.$$ Here $*$ denotes either the whole-space potential and homogeneous low-order norm, or a torus potential at its current scale and the regularized low-order norm in <a href="#lin:YL" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="lin:YL">[lin:YL]</a>. The constants are uniform for all sufficiently large current torus scales. The potential acts componentwise on the ordered tensor in the second term.

There are $c>0$, $C<\infty$, and $R_0<\infty$ such that every whole-space linear solution, and every torus linear solution with sufficiently large starting scale, satisfies $$\label{lin:energy-observation}
 \frac{d}{ds}\|v(s)\|^2
 \le-c\|v(s)\|^2+C\|v(s)\|_{L^2(B_{R_0})}^2.$$ The derivative can equivalently be understood through the integrated inequality. The constants are independent of the torus scale and of the slab length. In particular, for $t\ge0$, $$\label{lin:integrated-observation}
 \|v(t)\|^2\le e^{-ct}\|v(0)\|^2
   +C\int_0^t e^{-c(t-s)}\|v(s)\|_{L^2(B_{R_0})}^2\,ds.$$

There is a bounded real-linear map $\pi:Y\to\mathbb R^{14}$ such that $$\label{lin:spectral-coordinates}
 \pi E=I,\qquad S(s)E=Ee^{s\Lambda},\qquad
 \pi S(s)=e^{s\Lambda}\pi\quad(s\in\mathbb R).$$ For some $C_S<\infty$ and $\eta>0$, $$\label{lin:stable-decay}
 \|S(s)v\|_Y\le C_Se^{-\eta s}\|v\|_Y,
 \qquad s\ge0,\quad \pi v=0.$$ Thus $E\pi$ is the symmetry spectral projection. Constants depend on the fixed profile, $a$, and $k$.

For each fixed $M>0$ the step maps $\mathcal F_L^{(M)}$ are uniformly bounded for all sufficiently large $L$. Writing $L'=Le^{M/2}$, $$\label{lin:coordinate-defect}
 \lim_{L\to\infty}
 \|\pi_{L'}\mathcal F_L^{(M)}-e^{M\Lambda}\pi_L\|
 _{\mathcal L(Y_L,\mathbb R^{14})}=0.$$ There exists $M_0<\infty$ such that for every fixed $M\ge M_0$ there is $L_0(M)<\infty$ for which $$\label{lin:torus-stable-contraction}
 \|(I-P_{L'})\mathcal F_L^{(M)}|_{\ker\pi_L}\|
       _{\mathcal L(Y_L,Y_{L'})}\le\frac18,
 \qquad L\ge L_0(M).$$ Moreover $\|(I-P_{L'})\mathcal F_L^{(M)}E_L\|\le C_M$. Any prescribed positive bound on the coordinate defect can be imposed by enlarging $L_0(M)$ after $M$ has been fixed. These bounds persist after restarting a step at any larger scale.*

*After identifying torus spaces by dilation, the maps above and the step propagator are continuous when the scale and the input vary continuously. This last assertion is strong continuity for varying inputs, and does not assert operator-norm continuity of Schrödinger time evolution.

There are $\delta>0$, $L_0<\infty$ and $C_M<\infty$ such that, for $L\geq L_0$, the perturbation equation $U=Q_s+v$ has a unique solution through the step whenever $\|v(0)\|_{Y_L}\leq\delta$. Its endpoint is $$v(M)=\mathcal F_Lv(0)+h_L(v(0)),$$ where $$\begin{aligned}
 \|h_L(0)\|&\leq C_M L^{-2-a},\label{nl:step-zero}\\
 \operatorname{Lip}_{\|v\|\leq d}h_L
   &\leq C_M(d+L^{-2-a})\qquad(0<d\leq\delta).
 \label{nl:step-lip}
\end{aligned}$$ During the step, $$\sup_{0\leq s\leq M}\|v(s)\|_{Y_{L_s}}
 \leq C_M\bigl(\|v(0)\|_{Y_L}+L^{-2-a}\bigr).$$
